====Usage notes====
Remember that Chinese grammar is more about reducing ambiguity and less about following rules. The other meanings of this word all stem from the 3rd pronoun and demonstrative meaning (except the "to go" meaning which is unrelated). It roughly means "him, it, this" > "his, its" > "'s"/qualifier. Compare {{och-l |其|he, his, its|id=2}} > Min Nan possessive 個. 

Juxtaposition alone can indicate possession or qualification in Chinese even without this word. The actual purpose of this word is to act like a comma to group the words before it as a noun clause and prevent what comes after from being interpreted as the object of a verb. Consider "上山道" whence "上之山道" (mountain road that is up there) and "上山之道" (road for climbing a mountain). 
Like {{m|zh|的}}, unambiguous and popular compounds like {{m|zh|食品}} drop the {{m|zh|之}}. Other grammatical characters also work by enforcing a grouping of words. Modern Chinese fossilizes this word and uses {{m|zh|的}} instead, which originated from either 之 or {{och-l|者|one who > relativiser}}, and took on the "'s"/qualifier meaning.

{{m|zh|之}} as a pronoun, functions like a noun determiner while {{m|zh|然}} is a verb determiner. Consider "食此藥！若不食'''之''', 將死" which would mean "Eat this medicine! If you don't eat '''this''' (if you eat some other medicine), [you] will die" and contrast "食此藥！不'''然'''(=若不食), 將死" which would mean "Eat this medicine! If [you] don't '''do this''' (or if you do something else), [you] will die."

