===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
According to the {{w|Shuowen Jiezi}}, a {{liushu|p|nocap=yes}} of a baby without arms. Compare with {{zh-l|*子}}, where the arms are visible. The Shuowen defines {{lang|zh|了}} as "the crossing of lower legs". 

In the {{w|Shuowen Jiezi Zhu}}, a commentary to the Shuowen Jiezi, it is said that the pictogram instead depicts entangled legs ({{zh-l|*了戾}}). 

Its relation with the meaning "to finish" is likely via sound loan, but an argument can be made about "entangled legs" → "tie"→ "finish".

Unrelated to [[亨]].

===Etymology 1===
Verb “to finish; to be completed” > ''perfective aspect particle'' ({{lang|zh|了₁}}, weakened form) > ''change-of-state modal particle'' ({{lang|zh|了₂}}). It eventually replaced classical {{och-l|矣}}. 

Two kinds of particle uses of {{lang|zh|了}} can be distinguished: the perfective aspect particle after verbs (conventionally written as {{lang|zh|了₁}}) and the sentence-final modal particle ({{lang|zh|了₂}}). It is generally accepted ({{zh-ref|Wu, 1998}}) that these two uses of {{lang|zh|了}} are derived from the concrete verb “to finish”. The grammaticalisation of this verb had become common in the [[Tang]] Dynasty, initially in the form of ‹verb + (object) + ''perfective'' {{lang|zh|了}}› to indicate the completion of an action.

The perfective particle subsequently underwent further grammaticalisation to become the sentence-final change-of-state modal particle; {{zh-ref|Liu (1985)}} has demonstrated that this last step may have involved the coalescence of sentence-final {{lang|zh|了}} with {{zh-l|*也}} in certain Mandarin dialects, as the pronunciations of {{lang|zh|了₁}} and {{lang|zh|了₂}} are distinct in these dialects, with {{lang|zh|了₂}} rhyming with {{lang|zh|也}}.

Contrary to the suggestion in {{zh-ref|Schuessler (2007)}}, this word is not related to {{ncog|vi|rồi}}, due to the non-existent correspondence between any given phonemes. {{cog|th|แล้ว||to be finished; already; then, afterwards}}, {{cog|lo|ແລ້ວ||to finish; to be completed; ''perfective particle''}} are loans from Chinese.

