===Etymology 2===
{{zh-forms|alt=互,乎,與,護,付,給,呼,後,候,𢗝,厚,被-“by”,讓-“to allow; to cause”,使-“to cause”,俾-“to cause”,度-Quanzhou}}
{{och-l|與|id=2}} is often considered to be the original character ({{zh-ref|Lin, 1998, 1999}}; {{zh-ref|Mei, 2007}}); [https://twblg.dict.edu.tw/holodict_new/iongji/pdf/annesia327pdf.pdf Taiwan's Ministry of Education] considers the cognate {{och-l|予|id=2}} to be the original character. In either case, this would be the same word as etymology 1.

Alternatively, {{zh-ref|Zhao (1991)}} argues that this is a {{der|zh|qfa-tak|-}} substrate, suggesting a borrowing from {{cog|tai-pro|*haɰꟲ||to give}}, whence {{cog|za|hawj||to give; to allow}} and {{cog|pcc|haec||to give; to allow}}. Note that the {{cog|tai}} word has been connected to {{cog|ltc|-}} {{ltc-l|許|gloss=to allow}} ({{zh-ref|Manomaivibool, 1975}}). On the other hand, {{zh-ref|Zheng (2008)}} considers both the {{cog|nan-hbl|-}} word and the {{cog|qfa-tak}} word to be derived from {{zh-l|*與}}.

Assuming {{zh-l|*與}} to be the etymon, there are also various theories on the development of the ''h-'' initial:
* {{cog|och|-}} {{IPAfont|/*ɡl/}} → {{IPAfont|/*ɡ/}} → {{IPAfont|/*ɣ/}} → {{IPAfont|/h/}} ({{zh-ref|Mei, 2007}});
* Fricativization of the {{IPAfont|/*l/}} initial in {{cog|och|-}}, possibly originating in the Chu dialect ({{zh-ref|Zheng, 2008}});
* {{IPAchar|/tʰ/}} (Quanzhou) → {{IPAchar|/h/}} (other varieties) ({{zh-ref|Lin, 1998}}; {{zh-ref|Zhou, 2006}}; Taiwan's Ministry of Education).

{{zh-ref|Sagart (2017c)}} considers {{l|zh|與}} to be a problematic etymon but agrees with the third theory on the development of ''h-'' above. He suggests that a possible trigger for the change from {{IPAchar|/tʰ/}} to {{IPAchar|/h/}} is that {{IPAchar|/tʰɔ/}} is often used with {{l|zh|乞}} {{IPAchar|/kʰit/}}, allowing {{IPAchar|/kʰit tʰɔ/}} to be reanalysed as {{IPAchar|/kʰit hɔ/}}.  This etymology was also independently proposed by {{zh-ref|Lien (2002)}}.

