===Etymology 1===
{{ja-kanjitab|yomi=k|ほとけ}}
{{IPAfont|*⟨poto<sub>2</sub>ke<sub>2</sub>⟩}} → *{{IPAchar|/pətəkəi̯/}} → {{IPAchar|/ɸotoke/}} → {{IPAchar|/hotoke/}}

From {{inh|ja|ojp|sort=ほとけ|-}}, most likely from {{der|ja|qfa-kor-pro|*Pwutukye}}, hence related to modern {{cog|ko|^부처}}, {{cog|mnc|ᡶᡠᠴᡳᡥᡳ}}; see the Proto-Korean entry for more.<ref>{{cite-book|first=Alexander|last=Vovin|chapter=Why Manchu and Jurchen Look So Un-Tungusic|title=Tumen jalafun secen aku. Manchu Studies in Honour of Giovanni Stary|editors=Alessandra Pozzi, Juha Janhunen and Michael Weiers|location=Wiesbaden|publisher=Harrassowitz|year=2006|chapterurl=https://www.academia.edu/1804227/Why_Manchu_and_Jurchen_Look_so_Un-Tungusic|pages=255-266}}</ref><ref>{{cite-journal|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/10.7817/jameroriesoci.134.4.689|title=The Awakened Lord: The Name of the Buddha in East Asia|first=Thomas|last=Pellard|journal=Journal of the American Oriental Society|year=2014|doi=10.7817/jameroriesoci.134.4.689}}</ref> The word was presumably disseminated by the {{w|Baekje}} people of southwestern Korea, who first introduced Buddhism to Japan. The first two syllables are ultimately from {{der|ja|sa|बुद्ध|tr=buddhá}}, via {{der|ja|ltc|-}} {{ltc-l|佛陀}} or some similar Sinitic form. Thomas Pellard speculates that the final element is an ancient Koreanic word for "king; lord".

The more traditional explanation still given in Japanese dictionaries is that the final ''ke'' element might be the Chinese-derived suffix {{ja-r|気|け|[[spirit]]}},<ref name="DJR"/><ref name=NKDHotoke>{{R:Nihon Kokugo Daijiten 2|仏}}</ref> but a Koreanic explanation is more plausible.

First attested in the ''{{w|Nihon Shoki}}'' of 720 {{CE}}.<ref name=NKDHotoke/>

