Corrupted form of {{och-l|佗}}, which is found in {{w|Zuozhuan}}. 

===Etymology 1===
Etymology not clear ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}). Perhaps cognate with {{och-l|誃|to separate}}.

The modern sense of "he, she, it" only emerged after the Han period in colloquial passages, and displaced classical {{och-l|其|id=2}} and {{och-l|之}} in Mandarin dialects ({{zh-ref|Pulleyblank, 1995}}).

The phonological development from Middle Chinese was irregular. The expected reflex is ''tuō'', which is sometimes used in literary contexts for the classical "other" sense, but in most Mandarin dialects it is read like ''tā''.

